ef=\"https://www.theguardian.com/fashion/2026/jul/17/adversarial-clothing-are-garments-designed-to-confuse-facial-recognition-systems-about-to-g..."}],"claims":[{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"ai-vendors-have-found-someone-to-pay-their-infrastructure-bills-you","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/16/ai-vendors-have-found-someone-to-pay-their-infrastructure-bills-you/5273712","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"AI vendors have found someone to pay their infrastructure bills: You","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"ai-vendors-have-found-someone-to-pay-their-infrastructure-bills-you","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/16/ai-vendors-have-found-someone-to-pay-their-infrastructure-bills-you/5273712","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/16/ai-vendors-have-found-someone-to-pay-their-infrastructure-bills-you/5273712","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"ai-vendors-have-found-someone-to-pay-their-infrastructure-bills-you","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/16/ai-vendors-have-found-someone-to-pay-their-infrastructure-bills-you/5273712","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"Forrester warns that customers should brace for bigger software bills next year as software and AI vendors raise prices and pile on usage charges. Working from a survey of more than 2,600 business and technology decision-makers, the tech research company said software budgets were expected to rise \"as vendors increase prices or add usage charges to pass their AI costs to customers.\" In the last six months, Anthropic, OpenAI, and GitHub have shifted some services away from flat-rate subscriptions toward usage-based billing, prompting cost concerns among users. Forrester added Microsoft to the list, citing its recent launch of the premium E7 license, which bolts M365 Copilot, Agent 365, and security tools onto E5. Last year, consultants Bain & Company estimated that the build cost for AI datacenters would hit $2 trillion by 2030. Forrester said that AI would drive increases in data and software spending, with 80 percent of decision-makers expecting those budgets to rise. Sharyn Leaver, chief research officer at Forrester, said: \"The organizations that outperform in 2027 won't be those that spend the most on AI. They'll be the ones that invest in the foundations that make AI effect...","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"ai-vendors-have-found-someone-to-pay-their-infrastructure-bills-you","sourceUrl":"https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/07/16/ai-vendors-have-found-someone-to-pay-their-infrastructure-bills-you/5273712","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"Thu, 16 Jul 2026 18:21:00 +0200","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"the-risk-of-weather-data-sabotage-is-rising","sourceUrl":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/17/1140622/weather-data-sabotage/","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"The risk of weather data sabotage is rising","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"the-risk-of-weather-data-sabotage-is-rising","sourceUrl":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/17/1140622/weather-data-sabotage/","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/17/1140622/weather-data-sabotage/","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"the-risk-of-weather-data-sabotage-is-rising","sourceUrl":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/17/1140622/weather-data-sabotage/","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"Every morning, airline dispatchers, grid operators, and farmers around the world make decisions based on the same thing: a weather forecast. While these forecasts are something that most people glance at for two seconds, weather predictions influence major strategic decisions in many industries, with real money, livelihoods, and even actual lives at stake. Farmers use them to determine which crop variety to sow, when to fertilize, how much to invest in irrigation infrastructure, and how long livestock should graze. Utilities use them to decide where to build solar and wind farms, as well as how to price wholesale electricity. Predictions are used to warn people about extreme weather and to trigger emergency response measures. More recently, weather predictions have become relevant for an emerging industry: prediction markets , where people bet money on all kinds of real-world events, including the weather. However, the temptation to manipulate weather data to get an edge in these markets, combined with a collective move toward data-driven AI weather forecasting, is starting to put the accuracy of weather predictions at risk. These risks are relatively manageable for now, but as...","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"the-risk-of-weather-data-sabotage-is-rising","sourceUrl":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/17/1140622/weather-data-sabotage/","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"Fri, 17 Jul 2026 08:57:32 +0000","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"building-the-foundation-for-an-autonomous-enterprise","sourceUrl":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/02/1138433/building-the-foundation-for-an-autonomous-enterprise/","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"Building the foundation for an autonomous enterprise","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"building-the-foundation-for-an-autonomous-enterprise","sourceUrl":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/02/1138433/building-the-foundation-for-an-autonomous-enterprise/","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/02/1138433/building-the-foundation-for-an-autonomous-enterprise/","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"building-the-foundation-for-an-autonomous-enterprise","sourceUrl":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/02/1138433/building-the-foundation-for-an-autonomous-enterprise/","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"Artificial intelligence may have captured the public imagination through chatbots and image generators, but some of its most consequential use cases are unfolding far from consumer-facing tools. In industries where physical infrastructure, operational continuity, and safety are paramount, AI is becoming a core operating layer. With its sprawling industrial systems and constant stream of operational data, the energy sector offers a glimpse into what that future could look like. At Woodside Energy, AI adoption did not begin with generative models or enterprise copilots. The company has spent years building predictive analytics, optimization systems, and machine learning tools across exploration, drilling, maintenance, and plant operations. â€œWeâ€™ve always had very large volumes of operational data coming from the equipment and the plants and the assets that we operate,â€ says the companyâ€™s vice president for digital Andrew Melouney. â€œThose have created really clear, quite high-value use cases for us.â€ That long-term investment in infrastructure and governance is now enabling a broader shift toward agentic AI systems that can support complex industrial workflows. Rather than replace h...","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"building-the-foundation-for-an-autonomous-enterprise","sourceUrl":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/02/1138433/building-the-foundation-for-an-autonomous-enterprise/","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"Thu, 02 Jul 2026 12:51:44 +0000","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"adversarial-clothing-are-garments-designed-to-confuse-facial-recognition-systems-about-to-go-mainstream","sourceUrl":"https://www.theguardian.com/fashion/2026/jul/17/adversarial-clothing-are-garments-designed-to-confuse-facial-recognition-systems-about-to-go-mainstream","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"â€˜Adversarial clothingâ€™: are garments designed to confuse facial recognition systems about to go mainstream?","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"adversarial-clothing-are-garments-designed-to-confuse-facial-recognition-systems-about-to-go-mainstream","sourceUrl":"https://www.theguardian.com/fashion/2026/jul/17/adversarial-clothing-are-garments-designed-to-confuse-facial-recognition-systems-about-to-go-mainstream","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://www.theguardian.com/fashion/2026/jul/17/adversarial-clothing-are-garments-designed-to-confuse-facial-recognition-systems-about-to-go-mainstream","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-18T04:08:20.911Z"},{"kind":"producing-conten